        "title": "Historical Background (1858–1946)",
        "content": {
          "cabinetmissionplan1946": "Proposed formation of a Constituent Assembly and laid framework for constitutional drafting.",
          "colonialcontext": "Following the Revolt of 1857, governance shifted from the East India Company to the British Crown under the Government of India Act, 1858. This centralized imperial authority and marked the beginning of constitutional developments in colonial India.",
          "globalinfluences": "American constitutionalism, British parliamentary traditions, Irish directive principles, and post-war human rights philosophy influenced Indian framers.",
          "governmentofindiaacts": {
            "1858": "Transferred authority to the British Crown.",
            "1909": "Introduced separate electorates under Morley-Minto Reforms.",
            "1919": "Introduced dyarchy under Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms.",
            "1935": "Established provincial autonomy and federal structure blueprint."
          },
          "nationalmovementinfluence": "The Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, gradually transformed from a moderate reform body to a mass political movement demanding self-rule.",
          "needforconstitution": "India required a sovereign, democratic, republican framework ensuring rights, federal balance, and social justice.",
          "regulatingact1773": "The Regulating Act of 1773 was the first parliamentary intervention by Britain to regulate the East India Company’s rule. It introduced centralized administration and laid foundations for constitutional governance."
